A banking transaction illustrates the flow. The core banking application, acting as a Kafka producer, publishes the transaction as an event to a topic, keyed by account or customer so that all events for that customer stay in order within one partition. A consumer (for example a Kafka Connect HDFS sink or a Spark Structured Streaming job) reads the topic and lands the events as files in HDFS, where each file is split into blocks and replicated across DataNodes. A scheduled PySpark job reads the raw files, filters and validates them, joins reference data, aggregates, and writes a curated table in Parquet format back to HDFS. Finally, an analyst’s dashboard issues SQL to Trino, which reads the curated Parquet files in place, without copying them into a separate warehouse, and returns the result.

Keep this narrative in your head; the explanation question in this course is essentially a request to write it down with the correct technical detail.

## **HDFS**

### **Design assumptions**

HDFS (Hadoop Distributed File System) was built for a specific workload: very large files, written once and read many times, stored on clusters of ordinary machines where hardware failure is expected rather than exceptional. Those assumptions explain every design decision below.

### **Architecture**

An HDFS cluster has one active **NameNode** and many **DataNodes**.

The NameNode is the master. It stores the file system metadata: the directory tree, the file names and permissions, the list of blocks that make up every file, and the map of which DataNodes currently hold each block. This metadata is kept in memory for speed and persisted to disk as an fsimage snapshot plus an edit log of changes. The NameNode does not store file contents and does not sit in the data path; when a client reads a file, the NameNode tells it which DataNodes to contact and the client then reads the blocks directly from those DataNodes.

The DataNodes are the workers. They store the actual blocks on local disks, serve read and write requests from clients, send a heartbeat to the NameNode every few seconds, and periodically send a block report listing every block they hold.

A **Secondary NameNode** exists in classic deployments, and its name misleads almost everyone. It is not a backup and cannot take over. It periodically merges the edit log into the fsimage so that the NameNode’s restart time stays manageable. High availability, when required, is provided by a **Standby NameNode** kept in sync through shared JournalNodes, with automatic failover managed by ZooKeeper.

### **Blocks and replication**

Files are split into fixed size blocks, 128 MB by default. A 1 GB file becomes eight blocks. Each block is stored on several DataNodes; the default **replication factor** is three, so that 1 GB file occupies 3 GB of raw disk across the cluster. Replica placement is rack aware: with three replicas, one goes on the writer’s rack and two on a second rack, which protects against the loss of an entire rack while keeping write traffic between racks low.

### **Surviving a DataNode failure**

When a DataNode stops sending heartbeats for roughly ten minutes, the NameNode marks it dead. Every block that lived on that node now has fewer replicas than the target. Because the NameNode knows exactly which blocks were on the dead node, it instructs the surviving DataNodes that hold the other copies to replicate those blocks to healthy nodes until each block is back at its replication factor. Clients that were reading from the dead node simply fail over to another replica. No data is lost, and no human intervention is required. This is the complete answer to “why can HDFS survive the sudden loss of a DataNode”: each block exists on multiple DataNodes (three by default), and the NameNode re replicates under replicated blocks automatically.

### **Useful commands**

hdfs dfs -ls /data/raw                       *# list a directory*
hdfs dfs -mkdir -p /data/curated             *# create directories*
hdfs dfs -put transactions.csv /data/raw/    *# upload from local disk*
hdfs dfs -cat /data/raw/transactions.csv **|** **head**
hdfs dfs -du -h /data                        *# space used*
hdfs fsck /data/raw -files -blocks -locations   *# blocks and their DataNodes*
hdfs dfsadmin -report                           *# live and dead DataNodes*

Running hdfs fsck on a file and reading the output is the quickest way to make the block and replica concepts concrete. Each block line shows its replication factor and the DataNode addresses that hold it.

## **Apache Kafka**

### **Core concepts**

Kafka is a distributed, append only, replicated log. Its vocabulary:

• A **broker** is one Kafka server; a cluster has several.

• A **topic** is a named stream of records, such as transactions.

• A topic is divided into **partitions**. Each partition is an ordered, immutable sequence of records stored on disk. Partitions are the unit of parallelism: a topic with twelve partitions can be written and read by up to twelve workers in parallel.

• Each record in a partition has an **offset**, a sequential integer that identifies its position. Offsets are per partition; offset 57 in partition 0 and offset 57 in partition 3 are unrelated records.

• A **producer** writes records to a topic. A record has an optional **key** and a value. When a key is present, the producer hashes it and uses the result modulo the partition count to select the partition, so all records with the same key land in the same partition. Without a key, records are spread across partitions round robin (or in batches).

• A **consumer** reads records. Consumers belong to a **consumer group**. Within a group, each partition is assigned to exactly one consumer, so the group as a whole reads every partition once, in parallel. Two different groups each receive every record independently. If a group has more consumers than the topic has partitions, the extra consumers sit idle.

• Each partition is **replicated** across brokers. One replica is the leader and handles reads and writes; the others follow. The set of replicas that are fully caught up is the in sync replica set (ISR).

• Records are kept according to a **retention** policy (for example seven days) regardless of whether they have been consumed, which is what allows consumers to replay history.

### **The ordering guarantee**

Kafka guarantees ordering **only within a single partition**. Records written to the same partition are read in the order they were written. Across partitions there is no ordering guarantee at all. This is why the key matters: keying by customer\_id guarantees that every event for one customer is processed in order, because they all share a partition, while different customers can be processed in parallel on different partitions. An examination answer that claims ordering “across the whole topic” is wrong.

### **Offsets and commits**

A consumer group records its progress by committing offsets to Kafka. If enable\_auto\_commit is true (the default), the client commits periodically in the background, which is convenient but means a crash can lose messages that were fetched and committed but not yet processed. Disabling auto commit and calling commit() manually after each message is processed gives **at least once** delivery: a message might be processed twice after a crash, but it is never silently lost. Processing that tolerates duplicates (an idempotent write keyed on txn\_id, for instance) turns at least once into effectively exactly once.

auto\_offset\_reset decides where a consumer starts when the group has no committed offset yet: earliest reads from the beginning of the partition, latest reads only new records.

### **Producer acknowledgements**

acks=0 means the producer does not wait for confirmation; acks=1 waits for the partition leader; acks=all waits until every in sync replica has the record. Financial systems use acks=all for durability.

### **What Kafka is for**

Kafka decouples systems. The core banking application does not need to know who consumes its events or whether they are online; it writes once to Kafka, and any number of consumers (fraud detection, HDFS landing, real time dashboards, audit) read at their own pace. The log is durable, so a consumer that is down for an hour catches up when it returns. This decoupling, with durability and replay, is the reason Kafka sits at the front of the pipeline.

## **Apache Spark and PySpark**

### **Architecture**

A Spark application has a **driver** process, which runs your script, builds the execution plan and coordinates work, and a set of **executors** on worker nodes, which run tasks and hold data in memory. A cluster manager (YARN, Kubernetes, or Spark’s standalone manager) allocates the executors. Spark can read from HDFS, object storage, Kafka, JDBC databases and local files.

### **DataFrames and lazy evaluation**

The DataFrame API is the one you will use. A DataFrame is a distributed table with a schema. Operations on it fall into two classes, and the distinction is examined directly.

**Transformations** describe a new DataFrame in terms of an existing one. They are **lazy**: nothing executes when you call them. Spark records them in a logical plan. Examples: select, filter (and its alias where), withColumn, withColumnRenamed, drop, distinct, groupBy followed by agg, join, orderBy and sort, union, limit, repartition, cache.

**Actions** force execution. They return a value to the driver or write data out, and only at that moment does Spark optimise the accumulated plan and run it on the executors. Examples: count, show, collect, take, first, head, foreach, reduce, and every write or save call.

|   |
| - |

Call

|   |
| - |

Type

|   |
| - |

Reason

|   |
| - |

df.filter(col("amount") > 1000)

|   |
| - |

Transformation

|   |
| - |

Returns a new DataFrame, runs nothing

|   |
| - |

df.select("customer\_id")

|   |
| - |

Transformation

